## Why the proposed eligibility description is insufficient

| Boundary | Actual source contract and decision |
| --- | --- |
| Meaning of native | NumericalPolicy's constructor takes a profile argument but stores individual switches. Explicit solve_refinement/recovery/hypersparse overrides remain possible. Even _native_primal_kernel tests only four switches, not solve_refinement, precision_boosting or lp_refinement. A profile label or that predicate is not an omission eligibility proof. cleanup uses the context policy when present. |
| Observer versus diagnostics | SimplexDiagnostics(observer=nothing) still records refactor_initial and, if kernel_timing=true, FTRAN/BTRAN/pricing calls. record_event! runs before the observer check. Omitting the finish also omits the event; preserving the event alone still does not preserve the kernel work records. A future optimization could deliberately report less actual work, but that would need an explicit changed-work contract. This step grants none. |
| Two clocks | Cleanup's outer guard uses context.time_limit_seconds; refinement without a caller guard uses workspace.options.time_limit with the shared start. Native defaults disable solve refinement, so this internal clock is not unconditionally polled by every native solve. Infinite context time alone does not cover explicit policy overrides or the later paths. Requiring both limits infinite would remove this particular deadline obstruction, not the remaining obligations. |
| Default state and precision | The retained constructor still allocates owned costs/bounds, slack basis/factor, scratch, zero primal/prices, unit weights and devex reference. Finish validates/mutates the mask, performs actual basis solves/pricing and may update cache/quality/checkpoint state. Zero storage is not an initialized point. BigFloat stored/ambient behavior and the separate precision initialization branch cannot be qualified by Float64 success. Unit weights are already cheap defaults; this proposal saves no weight construction. |
| Failure, ownership and work | Ordinary cleanup keeps workspace=nothing until initialize_workspace returns. A numerical exception during finish reaches the outer catch with that value; the result uses inherited counters and _recover_original_failure(::Nothing) returns it. Early assignment of a raw workspace changes this ownership boundary. For a strictly ordinary policy with precision boosting/refinement disabled, the workspace recovery overload also returns the numerical result; early assignment alone is therefore NOT proof of a changed recovery outcome in that narrower subset. What remains unproved is that omitted arithmetic cannot fail, or that a new path preserves the first failure/message and all already consumed work. No new failing fixture is claimed. |
| Restored basis and logging | The basis is copied, validated and refactored; refactor_other is emitted BEFORE the two reconstruction solves. @logmsg runs even when verbose=false if the logger enables that level; caller exceptions are recorded in the shared guard and rethrown. Progress logging and projection/fallback occur later. A shortcut must preserve these positions and provenance; disabling an observer does not disable a caller logger. Retrying old initialization only after a changed restored path fails can duplicate work and select a different first exception. It is not an accepted fallback. |

Source anchors are simplex.jl::_initialize_workspace_state/
_finish_workspace_initialization!/recompute!, solver.jl::cleanup_original/
_context_numerical_policy, simplex_recovery.jl::_checked_basis_solve!/
_basis_solve_stop, simplex_precision_recovery.jl::_recover_original_failure,
simplex_diagnostics.jl::_diagnostic_event!, and hypersparse_pipeline.jl.
